An isothetic (axis-aligned) rectangle in the plane is a closed rectangle with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Let $\mathcal{R} = \{R_1, \ldots, R_n\}$ be a finite collection of isothetic rectangles with pairwise disjoint interiors. The rectangle visibility graph of $\mathcal{R}$ is the simple graph $G$ on vertex set $\{R_1, \ldots, R_n\}$ in which two distinct rectangles $R_i$ and $R_j$ are adjacent if and only if there exists an axis-aligned open rectangle $B$ of positive (finite) width such that:
(i) $B$ is either horizontal (its long axis parallel to the $x$-axis) or vertical (its long axis parallel to the $y$-axis);
(ii) $B$ meets $R_i$ and $R_j$ along opposite sides and is otherwise disjoint from $R_i \cup R_j$;
(iii) $B$ has empty intersection with the interior of every other rectangle $R_k \in \mathcal{R}$, $k \ne i, j$.

A simple graph $G$ is called a rectangle visibility graph if it arises as the rectangle visibility graph of some such collection $\mathcal{R}$.

Problem: Give a complete combinatorial characterization of the class of rectangle visibility graphs, i.e., determine necessary and sufficient conditions on a finite simple graph $G$ for $G$ to be a rectangle visibility graph.

